3 #uuid "d5a7b240-4502-5734-91ee-74717d793473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119076 #edb/ref 1080 "images/33/33be815edc07fd7da9958657505019fbb26b62d191dc1fbaf1eef6d21ae6f6d2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119076 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119076 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15bdf8da-b6a3-54f3-b627-0dbf6b959f5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119077 #edb/ref 1080 "images/fb/fb8401f02b6637bb031419d143c1399b66e22c34e292b288762a8fd25f5a55da.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119077 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119077 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "76c1bb18-5ecb-57e9-9c6b-e9c460b1555f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119567 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119567 #edb/ref 1071 "Construct a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $105^\\circ$ in the standard position." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "927b97a7-437e-5c46-b83d-1e8d2d62007e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119567 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/46/46e41d84cb00ea2a95f3d1c94e75863707fddf78ea7117de1ed415b71e66f2f1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119568 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119568 #edb/ref 1071 "Construct a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ in the standard position." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02cd545d-7fde-520b-924e-5ff4f8e4244b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119568 #edb/ref 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi} 5\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t72^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $72^\\circ.$ \n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119569 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119569 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50e22612-2f22-5c48-a2da-a6a0580375a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119569 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119570 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119570 #edb/ref 1071 "How is the angle $150^\\circ$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98f445f6-806d-5169-81f9-183b93e5bb94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119570 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "24d0f296-256c-5da4-8992-e2112dfe99b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Angles in Radians in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6435eb94-76f8-5e46-b005-03451ab6eba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921